WEBVTT

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:12.594
Drive past a wind farm. The blades turn, and somewhere behind them, electricity comes out.

00:00:12.894 --> 00:00:18.825
Nothing in chapters 22 to 29 explains how a turning shaft becomes a current.

00:00:19.125 --> 00:00:28.298
That is what chapter 30 is about, and the whole chapter fits in one sentence: a magnetic flux that changes, induces an emf.

00:00:29.548 --> 00:00:37.072
Section 30.1. Start with Faraday's first experiment: a loop of wire, an ammeter, and no battery.

00:00:37.372 --> 00:00:40.168
Hold the magnet still: nothing happens.

00:00:40.468 --> 00:00:43.145
Push it toward the loop: the needle swings.

00:00:43.445 --> 00:00:46.454
Pull it away: the needle swings the other way.

00:00:47.004 --> 00:00:54.774
What matters is not the magnet, and not the field. It is the magnetic flux through the loop, and whether that flux is changing.

00:00:55.074 --> 00:00:58.643
The flux through a flat loop is B, A, cos theta.

00:00:58.943 --> 00:01:05.057
So there are exactly three knobs: change B, change the area A, or change the angle theta.

00:01:05.607 --> 00:01:12.736
Faraday's law puts a number on it: the emf equals minus N, times the rate of change of the flux.

00:01:13.036 --> 00:01:19.183
N is the number of turns, and the minus sign is the whole subject of section 30.3.

00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:27.496
Pause and predict. The field through this coil rises in a straight line, then decays. When is the emf largest?

00:01:30.996 --> 00:01:37.932
The emf follows the slope, not the height. While B rises in a straight line, the emf is constant.

00:01:38.232 --> 00:01:42.734
At the peak it flips sign, and in the decay it fades away with the curve.

00:01:43.284 --> 00:01:54.200
The pitfall: a large flux does not mean a large emf. A loop sitting in a strong, steady field induces nothing at all. Only the rate of change counts.

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:58.680
A flux that changes. Nothing else induces an emf.

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:05.539
Section 30.2. Now keep the field steady, and move the conductor instead.

00:02:05.839 --> 00:02:14.960
The free electrons in the rod are moving charges in a magnetic field, so each one feels a force q v B, and they pile up at one end.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:21.636
They pile up until the electric force balances the magnetic one: q E equals q v B.

00:02:21.936 --> 00:02:29.311
Across a rod of length ell, that gives a potential difference of B ell v. This is the motional emf.

00:02:29.861 --> 00:02:36.852
Close the circuit with two rails and a resistor, and a current flows: I equals B ell v over R.

00:02:37.152 --> 00:02:46.208
Faraday's law agrees. The area enclosed grows at the rate ell v, so d phi d t is B ell v. Same answer, two routes.

00:02:46.758 --> 00:02:54.238
Pause and predict. You pull the bar at a steady speed, and the resistor gets warm. Where does that energy come from?

00:02:57.738 --> 00:03:06.359
From your hand. The current in the bar sits in the field, so the bar feels a magnetic force, I ell B, that opposes the motion.

00:03:06.659 --> 00:03:16.530
To keep a steady speed you push with exactly that force, and your power, F v, equals I squared R. Every joule in the resistor came from you.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:29.729
The pitfall: doubling the speed doubles the emf and the current, but it doubles the force too. So the power goes up four times, not two. Quick Quiz 30.2: 2F, and 4P.

00:03:30.079 --> 00:03:34.740
B ell v, twice over, and the energy comes from whoever is pushing.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:47.401
Section 30.3. Lenz's law is the minus sign of Faraday's law, said in words: the induced current creates a field that opposes the change in flux.

00:03:47.701 --> 00:03:59.899
Push the north pole toward the loop. The flux through the loop increases, so the induced current makes a field pointing back at the magnet. The loop shows a north face to the north pole, and repels it.

00:04:00.199 --> 00:04:12.084
Pull it away. The flux decreases, so the induced field now points the same way as the magnet's, to keep the flux up, and the loop attracts the magnet. Either way, the loop fights you.

00:04:12.634 --> 00:04:22.606
Pause and predict. Suppose the opposite were true, and the induced current helped the change. What would happen to a bar on rails, given one small push?

00:04:26.106 --> 00:04:35.034
It would speed up by itself, forever. More speed, more flux change, more current, more force, more speed. Kinetic energy from nothing.

00:04:35.334 --> 00:04:43.709
That breaks the conservation of energy. So Lenz's law is not an extra rule; it is energy conservation, in disguise.

00:04:44.259 --> 00:04:54.419
A loop crossing a region of field tells the whole story. The flux rises as it enters, stays flat while it is fully inside, and falls as it leaves.

00:04:54.719 --> 00:05:00.094
The emf, and the force on the loop, exist only during the entry and the exit.

00:05:00.644 --> 00:05:15.930
The pitfall: Lenz's law does not say that the induced field opposes the external field. It opposes the change. When the flux is decreasing, the induced field points along the external one, helping it, not fighting it.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:18.396
Oppose the change, not the flux.

00:05:19.746 --> 00:05:28.373
Section 30.4. Something has been hiding. In a loop sitting in a changing field, what actually pushes the charges around?

00:05:28.673 --> 00:05:34.207
Not a magnetic force: the charges are at rest. It must be an electric field.

00:05:34.507 --> 00:05:43.322
A changing magnetic field creates an electric field, in space, with or without a wire. The loop was only ever a detector.

00:05:43.872 --> 00:05:58.049
The work this field does per unit charge around the loop is the emf. So Faraday's law takes its general form: the line integral of E around any closed path equals minus d phi d t through it.

00:05:58.599 --> 00:06:10.982
For a long solenoid with a current that varies in time, the induced field circles the axis. Inside, it grows in proportion to r. Outside, it falls as one over r.

00:06:11.532 --> 00:06:20.207
Pause and predict. Around a closed path, the electrostatic field of chapter 23 does zero work. What about this one?

00:06:23.707 --> 00:06:35.771
Not zero. This induced electric field is non-conservative. Its field lines close on themselves, with no charges at their ends, and there is no potential you can assign to it.

00:06:36.071 --> 00:06:49.824
The pitfall, and Pitfall Prevention 30.1 in the book: never write V equals minus the integral of E for an induced field. Potential belongs to charges at rest, not to a changing B.

00:06:50.174 --> 00:06:52.572
The loop was only ever a detector.

00:06:53.922 --> 00:07:07.405
Section 30.5. Rotate a coil in a field, and you change theta continuously. The flux is N B A cos omega t, so the emf is N B A omega, sine omega t.

00:07:07.705 --> 00:07:15.514
This is the AC generator. Slip rings hand the ends of the coil to the outside world, so the output alternates.

00:07:15.814 --> 00:07:24.366
The peak, N B A omega, comes when the plane of the coil is parallel to the field: that is where the flux is changing fastest.

00:07:24.916 --> 00:07:35.771
Swap the slip rings for a split commutator, and the connections reverse every half turn. The output pulsates but never changes sign: the DC generator.

00:07:36.321 --> 00:07:46.297
Pause and predict. Which of these four does not raise the emf: lower resistance wire, faster spin, a stronger magnet, or more turns?

00:07:49.797 --> 00:08:01.101
Lower resistance. It may raise the current into a load, but the emf is N B A omega: four symbols, and R is not one of them. Quick Quiz 30.4.

00:08:01.651 --> 00:08:14.049
Now run it backwards. Feed a current in, and the coil turns: a motor. But a turning coil in a field is also a generator, so it produces a back emf that opposes the supply.

00:08:14.349 --> 00:08:18.329
The faster the motor spins, the smaller the current it draws.

00:08:18.679 --> 00:08:30.990
The pitfall, and the reason a jammed saw burns out: at zero speed there is no back emf, so the motor draws its full stalled current, and the heating goes as I squared R.

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:35.084
A generator run backwards is a motor, and it fights you.

00:08:36.434 --> 00:08:38.905
Five things to take from chapter 30.

00:08:39.205 --> 00:08:45.229
One: only a changing flux induces an emf. Three knobs: B, A, and theta.

00:08:45.529 --> 00:08:52.533
Two: a conductor moving through a field has an emf B ell v, and the energy comes from the pusher.

00:08:52.833 --> 00:08:58.481
Three: Lenz. The induced current opposes the change, because energy is conserved.

00:08:58.781 --> 00:09:03.447
Four: a changing B makes a non-conservative E, wire or no wire.

00:09:03.747 --> 00:09:12.261
Five: a rotating coil is a generator, N B A omega sine omega t, and a motor is the same machine, fighting back.

00:09:12.911 --> 00:09:20.158
The mind map and ten self-check questions for this chapter are on the course site. See you in chapter 31.
